# No LLM sidenotes in contracts (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-17), verbatim intent:

> **Please stop the behavior of inserting prompt information into the
> contract such as with: "Designation of IGA is not a claim that IGA is
> shipping or live. PUM and IGA designations do not invent features."
> Never comment in the contract text about our bad behavior as you just
> did. Review the text for such unacceptable LLM comments and sidenotes
> that are now prohibited.**

Then: add a hard rule against inserting LLM spurious comments.

## The rule

> **Never insert prompt information, agent-thinking, self-policing
> asides, or large-language-model (LLM) guardrail commentary into
> partner-facing or customer-facing legal or contract text. Write real
> legal allocation in counsel voice. Do not narrate internal drafting
> rules or "our bad behavior" in the paper the counterparty signs.**

## Scope

Operative surface: partner and customer agreements under
`pam-platform-docs/docs/go-to-market/contracts/` and any future
customer or partner agreement (End User License Agreement (EULA),
Managed Service Provider (MSP) Agreement, Reseller, Alliance,
Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) schedule, Non-Disclosure
Agreement (NDA), and related schedules).

| Surface | LLM sidenotes / prompt commentary? |
| --- | --- |
| Partner or customer legal text (EULA, MSP, Reseller, Alliance, OEM, NDA, schedules) | **No** |
| Word / Portable Document Format (PDF) fills generated from those templates | **No** |
| Cursor rules, `docs/dev`, commit bodies, agent prompts | **Yes** - drafting guidance stays here |
| Admin Single-Page Application (SPA) / operator UI | Out of scope here. AI thinking in UI is already banned by [`no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc`](no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc). This file is the legal-pack twin; do not duplicate that UI rule. |

## Forbidden

Do not paste any of the following into contract bodies, recitals,
definitions, schedules, or exhibits:

- Prompt restatements and self-policing asides, including
  "Designation of Identity Governance and Administration (IGA) is not
  a claim that IGA is shipping or live. Privileged User Management
  (PUM) and IGA designations do not invent features."
- "This Section does not claim Met / certified / assessment-ready"
  when that sentence exists only to remind the model
- "do not invent features," "roadmap only," "schema-only,"
  "we must not say X," `open-q-*`, "TODO for agents"
- Narration of Cursor rules, honesty vocabulary, or delivery status
  (Live / Partial / Absent / BLOCKED) as if it were a contract term
- Comments about prior agent mistakes or "our bad behavior"

Legal allocation is still required. Write it as counsel would:

- Customer may need frameworks or assessments.
- Provider licenses software. Provider does not warrant that
  Customer's use will result in any certification, Met finding, or
  assessment outcome.

That is a warranty and risk allocation. It is not a note to the model.

## Required agent behavior

1. Draft and edit contract prose in counsel voice only. Product-family
   designations (Privileged Identity Management (PIM), Privileged
   Access Management (PAM), PUM, IGA, and others) may appear as labels.
   Do not append a meta sentence that explains an internal drafting
   rule.
2. Before finishing a contract edit, search the touched paper for
   prompt leakage (invent features, shipping or live, schema-only,
   open-q, TODO for agents, Met / certified as a model reminder).
   Delete any hit. Do not replace it with a softer sidenote.
3. Keep drafting guidance in this rule, `docs/dev`, or the commit
   body. Never copy it into the signed instrument.
4. Mirror this file to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same
   change set ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)). The
   workspace `.cursor` directory is not a git root.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (prompt leakage left in the Go-to-Market (GTM) contract
pack):

```text
The designated product families include Privileged User Management
(PUM) and Identity Governance and Administration (IGA).
Designation of IGA is not a claim that IGA is shipping or live.
PUM and IGA designations do not invent features.
```

A counterparty reads the last two sentences as the Provider arguing
with itself. They are agent guardrails, not legal terms.

**Fix** (product designations only; warranty allocation in counsel
voice, no meta):

```text
The designated product families include Privileged User Management
(PUM) and Identity Governance and Administration (IGA). Listing a
family does not require every Customer to license every module. A
designation is a product-family label. It is not a statement that
every listed capability is included in every Order. Provider does
not warrant that Customer's use of the Software will result in any
certification or assessment outcome.
```

